From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Mon, 22 Sep 2026 09:12:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: the desktop has a theme, and its clients follow it

Registers the fourth member of the control protocol that crosses in BOTH
directions: DomicileHost.setTheme() goes down, and a `theme` event comes back
up to every chrome on the desk.

Three lists Chromium owns, and nothing else. The interface, the event, the
event init dictionary, the enum and the two codecs are all new files under
src/, which is most of this fork -- what cannot be a new file is a name in a
generated-bindings list and a name in the event registry.

WHY A PAGE CANNOT ANSWER THIS FOR ITSELF, since prefers-color-scheme looks
like it already does. That media query reports this engine's own notion of a
system preference, and under Domicile there is no system above the desktop to
have one: the shell IS the desktop's chrome, and what it paints in comes from
`[theme] mode` in the compositor's config and from the toggle on its own top
bar. Reading the media query would be a page asking the engine what the engine
was told, with a second place for the answer to be wrong.

AND WHY IT GOES BACK DOWN rather than staying in the renderer. The compositor
is the only process the desk's *clients* can hear -- it answers the settings
portal that GTK, Qt, Electron and Firefox read a color scheme from, out of this
same value. A theme that lived in the page would be a desktop whose panels went
dark while every window stayed light.

DomicileTheme is a WebIDL enum rather than the DOMString DomicileDisplay's
transform is, and the asymmetry is the direction: a transform is only ever
read, so the bindings have nothing to refuse, where setTheme() is *called* and
a DOMString would carry a word that is not a theme all the way to the socket.
The enum registers in generated_enumeration_sources_in_modules beside
DomicileCursorShape, which is the other member that earned one.

The mapping in both directions is the X-macro in
components/domicile/common/theme.h and nothing else, so
scripts/test-themes-agree.sh can compare all four writings of the set --
domicile_config::ThemeMode, domicile_protocol::Theme, this header's list and
mojom::Theme -- for membership and order without building anything.

Adding a member costs a near-full Blink rebuild, because a new inbound message
needs a new event type and that invalidates the generated bindings.
